I have all the information needed. Here is my thorough report.

## Summary of findings

All code lives under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src`, a Spring/OfficeFloor PetClinic variant. `customerCode` appears in exactly 4 Java files plus one OfficeFloor wiring YAML. There are NO repository methods that query owners by city anywhere in the repo.

---

### 1. Where `customerCode` is generated (format logic)

File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`

Class `AssignCustomerCode`, method `service`. Format is `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (upper-cased first 3 letters of last name, dash, 4-digit zero-padded global sequence = current owner count + 1):

```java
public class AssignCustomerCode {

    public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
        String lastName = owner.getLastName();
        String prefix = lastName.substring(0, Math.min(3, lastName.length())).toUpperCase();
        int sequence = ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1;
        owner.setCustomerCode(String.format("%s-%04d", prefix, sequence));
    }
}
```

Wiring / execution order — File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` (POST /api/owners flow). The relevant chain: `build` → `defaultRegistrationDate` → `assignCustomerCode` → `assignHousehold` → `countNamesakes` → `save` → `respond`:

```yaml
assignCustomerCode:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.AssignCustomerCode
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: assignHousehold
```

Because it runs before `save`, the `findAll().size()` count excludes the owner being created.

Storage — File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` (line 58-59):

```java
@Column(name = "customer_code")
private String customerCode;
```

with getter/setter `getCustomerCode()` / `setCustomerCode()` (lines 110-116).

---

### 2. Where membership number is derived from `customerCode`

File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`

The DTO field `membershipNumber` is populated by a MapStruct expression mapping (line 23) calling the default method `membershipNumber(owner)`. Format is `<customerCode>-M<YY>` where YY = last two digits of the registration year (e.g. `SMI-0007-M26`):

```java
@Mapping(target = "membershipNumber", expression = "java(membershipNumber(owner))")
```

```java
default String membershipNumber(Owner owner) {
    if (owner == null || owner.getCustomerCode() == null || owner.getRegistrationDate() == null) {
        return null;
    }
    return String.format("%s-M%02d", owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100);
}
```

---

### 3. Other values derived from `customerCode`

Within the same `OwnerMapper` there are other derived DTO fields, but note: only `membershipNumber` derives from `customerCode`. The others derive from name/email/namesakeCount, NOT from customerCode:

- `displayName` (from last/first name), `initials` (from name), `membershipTier` (from `namesakeCount` + email). Code (OwnerMapper.java lines 21-62):

```java
@Mapping(target = "displayName", expression = "java(displayName(owner))")
@Mapping(target = "initials", expression = "java(initials(owner))")
@Mapping(target = "membershipNumber", expression = "java(membershipNumber(owner))")
@Mapping(target = "membershipTier", expression = "java(membershipTier(owner))")
OwnerDto toOwnerDto(Owner owner);

default OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum membershipTier(Owner owner) {
    if (owner == null) {
        return null;
    }
    Integer namesakeCount = owner.getNamesakeCount();
    boolean uniqueName = namesakeCount != null && namesakeCount == 0;
    boolean hasEmail = owner.getEmail() != null && !owner.getEmail().isBlank();
    return uniqueName && hasEmail ? OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum.SILVER : OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum.BRONZE;
}
```

Related sibling Owner fields set by neighboring flow functions (not derived from customerCode, but part of the same create pipeline and worth noting):
- `namesakeCount` set in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/CountNamesakes.java` (`CountNamesakes.service`).
- `householdId` set in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignHousehold.java` (`AssignHousehold.service`), using `HouseholdNormalizer`.

So: the ONLY value derived from `customerCode` is `membershipNumber`.

---

### 4. Tests referencing customerCode / membership

File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` (entire file)

Note the class Javadoc describes a pending change ("customer-code-city") to a NEW format `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`, and the test asserts that new three-segment format — which the current `AssignCustomerCode` (only `<LAST3>-<NNNN>`) does NOT yet produce:

```java
/** customer-code-city: Change the customerCode format to '<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>' where CITY3 is the upper-cased... */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreCustomerCodeHasCityPrefix() throws Exception {
		int id = createOwnerOk(ownerNode());
		JsonNode n = fetchOwner(id);
		assertTrue(n.get("customerCode").asText().matches("[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}")); // <CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>
	}
}
```

File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — the base class only mentions "membership number" in a Javadoc comment (line 37) explaining the count/quota rules seen under `@Transactional`; no direct customerCode assertion. It provides helpers used by the test: `ownerNode()` (builds a valid owner with `firstName`, `lastName`=uniqueLastName, `address`, `city`=uniqueCity, `telephone`), `createOwnerOk`, `fetchOwner`, `extractId`. Key helper `ownerNode()` (lines 117-125):

```java
protected ObjectNode ownerNode() {
    ObjectNode o = om.createObjectNode();
    o.put("firstName", "Test");
    o.put("lastName", uniqueLastName());
    o.put("address", uniqueAddress());
    o.put("city", uniqueCity());
    o.put("telephone", uniqueTelephone());
    return o;
}
```

`uniqueCity()` returns `"Town" + seq()` (lines 94-96) — a 4+ char city, so a `<CITY3>` prefix would be the first 3 letters upper-cased ("TOW").

---

### 5. The Owner entity — lastName, city, id fields, and city query methods

`id` field — File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/BaseEntity.java` (Owner → Person → BaseEntity):

```java
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
protected Integer id;

public Integer getId() { return id; }
public void setId(Integer id) { this.id = id; }
```

`lastName` (and `firstName`) — File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Person.java` (superclass of Owner):

```java
@Column(name = "last_name")
@NotEmpty
protected String lastName;

public String getLastName() { return this.lastName; }
public void setLastName(String lastName) { this.lastName = lastName; }
```

`city` — File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` (lines 42-44, getter/setter 78-84):

```java
@Column(name = "city")
@NotEmpty
private String city;
...
public String getCity() { return this.city; }
public void setCity(String city) { this.city = city; }
```

Repository methods for querying owners by city — NONE. Confirmed:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java` defines only: `findByLastName(String)`, `findByLastName(String, Pageable)`, `findById(int)`, `save(Owner)`, `findAll()`, `findAll(Pageable)`, `delete(Owner)`. No city-based method.
- A repo-wide grep for `findByCity` / `ByCity` returned zero results.
- City only appears in JDBC SQL SELECT column lists (e.g. `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/jdbc/JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java` lines 86, 101, 125, 160, 186, 201) and in an UPDATE set clause — never in a WHERE filter by city.

To get owners for a given city today, callers must use `findAll()` and filter in memory on `getCity()` (this is exactly how `AssignCustomerCode` would need to count city members if the pending `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` change required per-city sequencing).

